</article>
{% endfor %}

<h2 class="pb"><span class="sec-no">6</span>How sites were chosen</h2>
<p>Two decisions had to be made explicit and defensible: which land is
<i>permissible</i>, and among permissible land, which is <i>preferable</i>. They are handled
separately and deliberately, because conflating them is how a ranking ends up recommending
forest land with an excellent score.</p>

<h3>Permissible — nine hard constraints, applied in order</h3>
<p class="small">The order matters. A cell is reported against the first constraint it fails, so
cheap and definitive tests come first and judgement calls come last. That way "rejected: inside
a RED zone" is never displaced by "rejected: too far".</p>
<ol class="chain" style="counter-reset:none;list-style:decimal;padding-left:1.4em">
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Zone RED</b> — inside a red zone declared by the district ruleset.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Zone ORANGE</b> — not permitted for new residential layout.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Residual hazard</b> — combined annual probability above the
      resettlement cap. A site is never risk-free; the cap states how much residual risk the
      state is prepared to accept, and it is printed on every funnel above.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Slope</b> — above the NDMA habitable-slope norm
      ({{ '%.0f'|format(norms|selectattr('0','equalto','safe_slope_deg')|map(attribute='1')|map(attribute='value')|first) }}°).</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Erosion</b> — land predicted lost inside the planning horizon.
      Moving a village onto a retreating shoreline is a 20-year problem, not a solution.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Inundation</b> — below the flood or surge screening level.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Land class</b> — forest, CRZ or private land not readily available.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Already built</b> — occupied at settlement density.</li>
  <li style="padding-left:0"><b>Distance</b> — beyond what households accept from their origin.
      Livelihood, school and kinship ties are why relocation colonies stand empty when this is ignored.</li>
</ol>
<p class="small muted">Surviving cells are grouped into contiguous parcels by hex adjacency
before ranking, because a site is a parcel: you cannot lay a road, a water line and a school
across cells that do not touch.</p>

<h3>Preferable — AHP weights, then TOPSIS ranking</h3>
{% if ahp %}
<figure>{{ figs.weights|safe }}
  <figcaption>Criteria weights from the principal eigenvector of the pairwise-comparison
  matrix (Saaty 1980). Consistency ratio <b>{{ '%.4f'|format(ahp.consistency_ratio) }}</b>
  against the 0.10 admissibility threshold, λ<sub>max</sub> =
  {{ '%.3f'|format(ahp.lambda_max) }} — {{ 'the judgement matrix is consistent' if ahp.acceptable else 'INCONSISTENT: the weights must be revised before use' }}.</figcaption></figure>
<p class="small">{{ ahp.note }}</p>
{% endif %}
<div class="banner calm">
  <b>Why fixed weights rather than a learned model</b>
  There is no labelled corpus of "correct" resettlement sites in India to train on — the
  historical record contains sites chosen for reasons no one wrote down, including some that
  failed. So the weights come from a structured expert judgement whose internal consistency can
  be tested and whose numbers a District Magistrate can change and re-run. A learned weighting
  would be more impressive and less accountable.
</div>

<h3>Acceptance rules</h3>
<ul class="tight small">
  <li>A parcel is accepted only above a TOPSIS closeness of
      <b>{{ acceptance.min_topsis_closeness }}</b> — a site cannot merely be the best of a bad set.</li>
  <li>It must hold at least <b>{{ acceptance.min_households_per_site }} households</b>; below
      that the per-household cost of a road, a water scheme and a school is indefensible.</li>
  <li>A portfolio is accepted only if it covers at least
      <b>{{ (acceptance.min_portfolio_coverage * 100)|round|int }}%</b> of the people at risk.
      Partial coverage is reported as a shortfall rather than hidden.</li>
</ul>
{% if figs.site_space %}
<figure>{{ figs.site_space|safe }}
  <figcaption>Every candidate parcel in the district: distance from its origin habitation
  against carrying capacity. Green accepted, orange rejected. The visible frontier is the real
  trade-off — nearby land is small, and large land is far.</figcaption></figure>
{% endif %}
{% if figs.binding %}
<figure>{{ figs.binding|safe }}
  <figcaption>What actually limits capacity across {{ n_sites }} candidate parcels
  ({{ n_accepted }} accepted). If water dominates this chart, the district's relocation
  programme is a water-supply programme.</figcaption></figure>
{% endif %}

<h2><span class="sec-no">7</span>Phasing under the budget</h2>
{% if phase_summary %}
<p>Habitations are funded tier-first, and within a tier by an <b>exact 0/1 knapsack</b> over
avoided loss subject to the annual budget of
{{ plan_meta.annual_budget_inr|inr }}. Greedy benefit-per-rupee selection was rejected because
it reliably funds four cheap medium-risk habitations ahead of one expensive IMMEDIATE one —
arithmetically efficient and indefensible in front of the people left behind.</p>
<figure>{{ figs.phases|safe }}
  <figcaption>Capital cost against present-value avoided loss, by phase. Benefit is discounted
  at the social rate over 30 years and counts avoided physical damage only.</figcaption></figure>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th class="n">Habitations</th><th class="n">Households</th>
    <th class="n">Persons</th><th class="n">Capital cost</th>
    <th class="n">PV benefit</th><th class="n">BCR</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for key, v in phase_summary.items() if key != 'unfunded' %}
    <tr><td>{{ key.replace('_',' ')|title }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ v.habitations }}</td><td class="n">{{ v.households|num }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ v.persons|num }}</td><td class="n">{{ v.cost_inr|inr }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ v.pv_benefit_inr|inr }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ (v.pv_benefit_inr / v.cost_inr)|dp2 if v.cost_inr else '—' }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  {% if phase_summary.unfunded.habitations %}
    <tr style="background:#fdf1f1"><td><b>Unfunded</b> within the horizon</td>
      <td class="n">{{ phase_summary.unfunded.habitations }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ phase_summary.unfunded.households|num }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ phase_summary.unfunded.persons|num }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ phase_summary.unfunded.cost_inr|inr }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ phase_summary.unfunded.pv_benefit_inr|inr }}</td><td class="n"></td></tr>
  {% endif %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% if phase_summary.unfunded.habitations %}
<div class="banner"><b>Unfunded need</b>
  {{ phase_summary.unfunded.habitations }} habitation(s) carrying
  {{ phase_summary.unfunded.persons|num }} people at risk do not fit inside
  {{ plan_meta.n_phases }} years of budget. They are listed rather than dropped, because an
  unfunded requirement is a decision the state should take knowingly.</div>
{% endif %}
{% else %}
<p class="nofig">No phasing computed for this district.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2 class="pb"><span class="sec-no">8</span>Where every number came from</h2>
<table>
  <caption>Input datasets in this snapshot. Synthetic rows are stand-ins produced by this
  system; they are physically plausible and they are not measurements.</caption>
  <thead><tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Role</th><th>Vintage</th><th class="n">Resolution</th>
    <th>Source</th><th>Licence</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for ds in datasets %}
    <tr{% if ds.synthetic %} style="background:#fdf6ef"{% endif %}>
      <td>{{ ds.name }}</td><td>{{ ds.role }}</td><td class="n">{{ ds.vintage }}</td>
      <td class="n">{{ ds.resolution }}</td><td class="small">{{ ds.source }}</td>
      <td class="small">{{ ds.licence }}</td>
      <td>{% if ds.synthetic %}<span class="flag">synthetic</span>{% else %}<span class="ok">authoritative</span>{% endif %}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="small">Snapshot <span class="mono">{{ d.snapshot_id }}</span> ·
  ruleset <span class="mono">{{ manifest.ruleset_version }}</span> ·
  grid backend <span class="mono">{{ manifest.grid.backend }}</span> ·
  created <span class="mono">{{ manifest.created_at }}</span>{% if manifest.code_git_sha %} ·
  code <span class="mono">{{ manifest.code_git_sha }}</span>{% endif %}. Re-running this
  snapshot id with the same ruleset reproduces every figure in this document.</p>

{% if model %}
<h3>Model card — landslide susceptibility</h3>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th style="width:26%">Form</th><td>L2-regularised logistic regression
      (<span class="mono">{{ model.kind }}</span>) on standardised terrain predictors</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Predictors</th><td class="mono small">
      {% for f in model.features %}{{ f }} β={{ '%+.3f'|format(model.coef[loop.index0]) }}{{ ' · ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}
      <br>intercept {{ '%+.3f'|format(model.intercept) }}
      (uncorrected {{ '%+.3f'|format(model.intercept_raw) }})</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Training set</th><td>{{ model.n_pos|num }} positive cells,
      {{ model.n_neg|num }} negative cells</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Discrimination</th><td>AUC {{ '%.4f'|format(model.auc_train) }} in-sample;
      <b>{{ '%.4f'|format(model.auc_cv) }}</b> under spatially blocked cross-validation.
      The blocked figure is the honest one — random k-fold on autocorrelated terrain leaks
      neighbouring cells across the split and inflates AUC.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Prevalence correction</th><td>King &amp; Zeng (2001) prior correction to a true
      prevalence of {{ model.true_prevalence|pct(2) }}. Without it a case-control sample
      ranks cells correctly and reports absolute probabilities that are wrong by an order of
      magnitude — and this system multiplies those probabilities by population, so the error
      would propagate straight into the relocation cost.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Attribution</th><td>Exact Shapley values for a linear logit,
      φ<sub>i</sub> = β<sub>i</sub>(z<sub>i</sub> − E[z<sub>i</sub>]) — closed-form, not
      sampled, so a per-cell explanation is reproducible to the last digit and sums exactly to
      the prediction. Standardisation means μ and σ are persisted with the coefficients;
      without them the saved model cannot be turned back into either a prediction or an
      attribution.</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Known limits</th><td class="small">Trained on a synthetic inventory in this
      snapshot: the coefficients are indicative of the direction and rough magnitude of terrain
      relationships, not calibrated to observed failures in {{ d.name }}. Substitute the GSI
      national landslide inventory before operational use. The model sees terrain only — it has
      no knowledge of lithology, joint orientation, road cuts or drainage interference, all of
      which govern real failures on the Joshimath slope.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endif %}
{% if gev %}
<h3>Extreme-value fit — design rainfall</h3>
<p class="small">{{ gev.distribution }} fitted to {{ gev.n_years }} years of annual maxima:
  location {{ gev.loc_mm|dp1 }} mm, scale {{ gev.scale_mm|dp1 }} mm,
  shape ξ = {{ gev.shape_xi|dp2 }}
  {%- if gev.shape_xi and gev.shape_xi > 0 %} (heavy-tailed — the largest events are
  substantially larger than a Gumbel fit would suggest, which is why a fitted shape parameter is
  used rather than assumed zero){% endif %}.
  Return levels: 25-yr {{ '%.0f'|format(gev.rl_25yr_mm) }} mm ·
  50-yr {{ '%.0f'|format(gev.rl_50yr_mm) }} mm ·
  100-yr {{ '%.0f'|format(gev.rl_100yr_mm) }} mm.
  Source: {{ gev.source }}{% if gev.is_synthetic %} <span class="flag">synthetic</span>{% endif %}.</p>
{% endif %}

<h3>Norms applied</h3>
<table>
  <caption>Every threshold in this report is a published Indian norm, cited. None is a
  parameter this system invented.</caption>
  <thead><tr><th>Norm</th><th class="n">Value</th><th>Unit</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {% for key, n in norms %}
    <tr><td>{{ key|title }}</td><td class="n">{{ n.value }}</td><td>{{ n.unit }}</td>
      <td class="small">{{ n.source }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2><span class="sec-no">9</span>What this analysis cannot do</h2>
<p>A screening tool that presents itself as a survey gets people killed. The following are
limits of method, not bugs, and they are stated here so that a reader can decide what weight to
put on each finding.</p>
<dl class="gloss">
  <dt>It is a screening, not a geotechnical investigation.</dt>
  <dd>Nothing here substitutes for borehole logs, a slope-stability analysis or a site
    inspection. The output identifies <i>where to send an engineer</i>, in what order.</dd>

  <dt>Population is disaggregated, not counted.</dt>
  <dd>Census village totals are redistributed within the village by building footprint area,
    storey estimate and probability of residential use, and the village total is conserved
    exactly. A cell-level count is therefore an expectation, not a roll of residents. Village
    totals are as good as the Census; cell values are as good as the footprint layer.</dd>

  <dt>Land availability is the weakest input.</dt>
  <dd>Ownership and land class drive the largest single exclusion in most funnels above, and it
    is the layer most likely to be a stand-in. A site on land that turns out to be private or
    forest is not a site. Revenue-record verification must precede any acquisition step.</dd>

  <dt>Hazard interaction is approximated.</dt>
  <dd>Perils are combined as independent annual probabilities with a coupling term for known
    cascades. Real cascades — a landslide-dammed river breaching, a cyclone on a saturated
    catchment — are correlated in ways a product of marginals understates. Where a coupling
    term applies it is reported per cell.</dd>

  <dt>No value is placed on human life.</dt>
  <dd>Every benefit-cost ratio in this report counts avoided physical damage only. Mortality is
    excluded deliberately: a value of statistical life makes relocation look profitable for
    reasons the state should decide politically, not arithmetically. Every BCR here is therefore
    a <b>lower bound</b> on the case for acting.</dd>

  <dt>Effectiveness is never assumed complete.</dt>
  <dd>No mitigation option is credited with eliminating risk, and no relocation is credited with
    zero residual hazard — the receiving site's own residual probability is carried through and
    printed.</dd>

  <dt>Consent is not modelled.</dt>
  <dd>Distance caps stand in for a social process they cannot replace. Households refuse to move
    for reasons — grazing rights, a shrine, a fishing landing, a school-going child — that no
    raster contains. Gram Sabha consultation is a precondition, not a downstream step.</dd>

  {% if d.contains_synthetic %}
  <dt>This snapshot contains synthetic inputs.</dt>
  <dd>Marked row by row in section 8. Figures are internally consistent and externally
    unverified. Publishing any habitation-level number from this snapshot as fact would be a
    misrepresentation.</dd>
  {% endif %}
</dl>

<h3>What would change these answers</h3>
<ul class="tight">
  <li>A real ownership and land-class layer would move sites in or out of the funnel at gate 7 —
      the single highest-leverage data substitution available.</li>
  <li>The GSI landslide inventory would replace the trained susceptibility surface and change
      cell-level probabilities, though the ranking of habitations is driven mainly by
      subsidence and slope, which are observed.</li>
  <li>CGWB block groundwater status and the JJM source register would replace the inferred water
      yield, which is the binding constraint on
      {{ (all_sites|selectattr('binding_constraint','equalto','water')|list|length) }} of
      {{ n_sites }} candidate parcels here — so it directly sets capacity.</li>
  <li>A district-agreed residual-hazard cap and travel-distance limit would replace the defaults
      used above. Both are policy choices, both are printed, and both can be changed and re-run
      in under a second per district.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="sec-no">10</span>Recommendation and approval</h2>
<p>Subject to field verification of the habitations in section 5, the district administration is
recommended to:</p>
<ol class="tight">
  {% if tier_counts[0].n %}
  <li>Issue notice and begin land acquisition for the
      <b>{{ tier_counts[0].n }} IMMEDIATE habitation(s)</b> covering
      {{ tier_counts[0].persons|num }} persons at risk, capital requirement
      {{ tier_counts[0].cost|inr }}, inside the current financial year.</li>
  {% endif %}
  {% if tier_counts[1].n %}
  <li>Commission geotechnical and revenue verification of the receiving parcels named for the
      <b>{{ tier_counts[1].n }} SHORT_TERM habitation(s)</b>
      ({{ tier_counts[1].persons|num }} persons, {{ tier_counts[1].cost|inr }}).</li>
  {% endif %}
  {% if tier_counts[3].n %}
  <li>Sanction in-place mitigation for the <b>{{ tier_counts[3].n }} MITIGATE habitation(s)</b>
      ({{ tier_counts[3].persons|num }} persons), where protection returns more avoided loss per
      rupee than relocation.</li>
  {% endif %}
  <li>Replace the synthetic inputs listed in section 8 and re-run this screening before any
      household is informed or any figure is published.</li>
</ol>

